findings.claim:
v2 keeps sections editable until PS-11, and REQ-ADR-002 allows additional parts 'throughout the entire process'. But once the request is at "Supplier: final release", only the supplier can move it:
- the RMM holds no step there;
- the plan forbids moving the request back into step 4, because I-1 would fire again;
- the plan makes G5 the point of no return (open decision 18).
So a value or document changed after G5, or an annex activated after G5, can be released and frozen without any department having reviewed it (E-P-50, E-P-67). The built steps can't express this v2 behaviour without a decision from BDF.

On cancellation (REQ-PROC-049) or manual completion (REQ-PROC-045), the cascade must end:
- the request;
- every section (created by RMM or by department users);
- every open query (created by department users);
- open change requests (created by the supplier).
Sections and queries that wait on the supplier have no BDF-held step, so a BDF role can't Reject them natively. The only routes are a withdraw by the user who created the item, or the Case Admin admin route. Both are unverified (K-13, MM §15).

The only route proven tonight is the admin DELETE, which removes the item entirely (404 afterwards). That loses the justification and the history that REQ-PROC-049 requires.

MT-12 as written can't settle the question. TEST-B-0001 is created with the API key, so Thomas is at the same time its initiator, a member of CIQP BDF Case Admin and the portal Admin. And MT-12 tests only a request, not a section or query created by another user.

findings.recommended_fix:
Rewrite MT-12 with separate identities, none of them portal Admin:
- an RMM-only test user creates the request;
- a department-only user creates a section and a query;
- a third internal user holds only CIQP BDF Case Admin.

Test:
- the initiator withdraws the request at a supplier step;
- an RMM who didn't create it tries to withdraw it (expected: refused);
- Case Admin withdraws or rejects the department-created section and query;
- the supplier's tasks disappear, and each item stays readable with the justification in its history.

Never use admin DELETE in the cascade. Record in plan §6.1 which identity the cascade must run as.

findings.claim:
Trade_Name, Supplier_Display_Name, Markets_Label (SF-11, owned by RMM per AS-06), Template_Label and BDF_Contact_Name/Email are editable only at "Issue request". RMM holds steps 2 and 4 but can't correct these fields there.

So three common changes can't reach the request, except through the unverified admin route:
- a new target market (E-P-58);
- a handover to another RMM owner (the supplier sees the responsible person, REQ-PROC-030);
- an accepted 'Metadata correction' change request.

Under v2, the supplier edits its own data directly until PS-11, so change requests mostly concern exactly these BDF-owned values. An accepted change therefore has nowhere to land.

The build log covers values written by our sessions ('sessions must validate Dept_Code'). But people also type routing values into native forms:
- Dept_Code at "Beiersdorf: triage" (Mandatory Text, typed by the RMM);
- Dept_Code and Response_Required on a CIQP Query created with '+ New' (every department holds InitiateItems).

A typo such as 'Reg', 'SAF ' or 'yes' matches no conditional step:
- a change request goes from triage straight to End (Completed), with no department assessment;
- a query skips "Supplier: respond". Or, with Response_Required = "Yes", it ends right after the supplier answers, with no review, so its traffic light shows closed (green). This second case is inferred from the verified 'no match → End' rule.

